    FireSafetyTopic(
      id: 'FLS-08',
      title: 'Emergency Lighting & Exit Signage',
      subtitle: 'Visibility, route identification, backup power and inspection',
      icon: Icons.lightbulb_rounded,
      accent: Color(0xFFB58A18),
      overview: 'Emergency lighting and exit signs support safe movement when normal lighting fails or visibility is reduced. They must make the approved escape route, changes of direction, exits and safety equipment identifiable. System type, coverage, illumination values, duration, monitoring and testing must be verified against the current adopted code, approved drawings and authority requirements.',
      sections: [
        FireSafetySection('System purpose & components', [
          'Emergency lighting may illuminate escape routes, stairs, changes in level, exits, safety equipment and designated areas requiring continued safe operation.',
          'Exit signs identify the approved direction and final exit. Signs must remain visible, correctly oriented and not hidden by temporary works, stored goods or decorative coverings.',
          'Emergency luminaires may be self-contained or supplied from a central battery/monitored system as approved by the design. Do not assume a particular system is acceptable without checking the approved fire strategy.',
          'Emergency lighting is not a substitute for clear routes, functioning alarms, evacuation training or safe building design.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Field inspection and testing', [
          'Check physical damage, cleanliness, indicator status where provided, obstruction, sign visibility, correct direction and evidence of unauthorized alteration.',
          'Functional tests, duration tests, battery replacement and electrical repairs must be performed by competent authorized personnel under the approved maintenance schedule.',
          'Record asset/location, test type, date, result, defect, interim control, owner and verified closure. A visual check alone does not prove required duration or illumination performance.',
          'If emergency lighting or exit signage is impaired, notify management, assess affected routes and occupancy, provide approved temporary measures or restrict use until safe arrangements are restored.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Technical values and compliance caution', [
          'DCD FAQ material describes emergency lighting objectives and gives specific illumination values and location guidance. These are source-specific provisions; verify current code edition, amendments, building type and project approval before using a numeric value as a compliance criterion.',
          'Do not copy generic international lux levels, duration, spacing or mounting heights into a UAE project as legal requirements without checking the adopted authority standard and approved design.',
          'Check the actual approved drawings, test certificates, manufacturer data and competent electrical/fire-system specialist findings.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Common hazards & controls', [
          'Failed battery or supply: emergency route becomes dark after normal power loss. Verify scheduled tests and repair failures promptly.',
          'Wrong or obscured arrow: occupants may be directed toward a dead end or hazard. Compare every sign with the approved evacuation drawing and actual exit.',
          'Construction changes: hoarding or new partitions may invalidate sign direction and lighting coverage. Review temporary arrangements before occupancy or work shift.',
          'Poor housekeeping, glare, dust, water ingress and damaged fittings can reduce reliability. Protect equipment without covering lenses or labels.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Stop-work & site example', [
          'Restrict occupancy or affected work if the approved escape route lacks required emergency illumination/signage and no authorized compensatory arrangement exists.',
          'Example: temporary corridor rerouting changes the exit direction. Obtain competent approval, relocate/add approved signs and lighting as designed, brief workers and document verification before using the route.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Detailed emergency-lighting and signage field checks', [
          'Emergency lighting supports safe movement when normal lighting fails; exit signs identify the approved escape direction and final exits. Confirm that installed type, location, coverage and performance match approved drawings and authority-approved design rather than selecting values by guesswork.',
          'Visual inspection: check signs are visible from the intended approach, arrows point along the approved route, luminaires are not obscured or damaged, lenses are clean, fittings are secure, and no temporary partition or stored item blocks visibility.',
          'Functional testing and duration testing must follow the adopted code, approved maintenance plan and manufacturer procedure, performed by competent authorized personnel. Do not invent a universal lux value or test interval; record the governing criterion and test method.',
          'During a test, coordinate occupants and operations, maintain safe alternative arrangements if required, identify affected areas, record start/end time and ensure normal supply and charging are restored afterward.',
          'If a fitting fails, mark the exact location, assess whether the escape route remains adequately protected, notify facilities/fire-system owner, provide approved interim controls or restrict occupancy/activity as directed, then retest and document closure.',
          'Keep asset IDs, location plans, inspection/test results, battery or fitting replacement records, defect reports, approvals and restoration evidence available for audit and Civil Defence/authority inspection.',
        ]),
      ],
      checklist: [
        'Emergency luminaires and exit signs are present where shown on approved drawings.',
        'Signs are visible, legible, correctly oriented and not obstructed.',
        'Emergency lights show normal/ready status where indicators are fitted.',
        'Testing and maintenance are current under the approved schedule.',
        'Battery, supply and functional/duration test defects are tracked to closure.',
        'Temporary route changes have matching approved signs and lighting.',
        'Impairments have risk assessment, notification and interim controls.',
      ],
      requiredDocuments: [
        'Approved emergency lighting and exit-sign drawings/schedule',
        'Functional and duration test records',
        'Maintenance, battery replacement and defect reports',
        'Fire system impairment and compensatory-control records',
        'Temporary escape-route change approval and briefing records',
        'Authority-approved fire strategy and applicable code edition reference',
      ],
      interviewQuestions: [
        FireSafetyQuestion('Why is emergency lighting required?', 'It supports identification and safe movement along escape routes and helps occupants locate exits and safety equipment when normal lighting fails.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Does a green indicator prove emergency lighting is fully compliant?', 'No. Check the approved inspection/test regime, functional and duration results, physical condition, coverage and records.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('What should you do if an exit sign arrow points to a blocked route?', 'Report and restrict the affected route as necessary; have the approved route/signage corrected by competent personnel and brief occupants before use.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Can a generic lux value be applied to every UAE project?', 'No. Verify the current authority-adopted code, occupancy, approved drawings and applicable project criteria.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Who should repair emergency lighting circuits?', 'A competent authorized electrical/fire-system technician following approved isolation, testing and maintenance procedures.'),
      ],
      references: [
        'Dubai Civil Defence FAQ on emergency lighting: https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/en/preventive-safety/rules-regulations/faq-uae-fire-and-life-safety-code-of-practice.jsp?limit=20&start=60',
        'Dubai Civil Defence emergency action plan annexure: https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/eng/ANNEXUREB2EMERGENCYEVACUATION.pdf',
        'Current UAE Fire and Life Safety Code, authority-approved drawings and project fire strategy.',
        'Manufacturer instructions and competent electrical/fire-system service records.',
      ],
    ),
    FireSafetyTopic(
      id: 'FLS-09',
      title: 'Fire Doors & Compartmentation',
      subtitle: 'Limit fire and smoke spread through barriers, doors and service penetrations',
      icon: Icons.door_front_door_rounded,
      accent: Color(0xFF8B5A3C),
      overview: 'Compartmentation uses fire-resisting walls, floors, doors, protected shafts and sealed service penetrations to limit fire and smoke spread and protect escape routes. Fire-resistance ratings and construction details are design-specific. Field personnel must preserve the approved arrangement and report damage or unauthorized openings.',
      sections: [
        FireSafetySection('Key terms & function', [
          'A fire compartment is a defined part of a building separated by fire-resisting construction to restrict fire spread for the design period.',
          'A fire door assembly may include the leaf, frame, seals, hinges, closer, latch, glazing and approved hardware; the assembly must remain compatible and correctly installed.',
          'Fire stopping seals gaps around cables, pipes, ducts and other services through fire-rated barriers. A fire door and a fire-stopping system are not interchangeable.',
          'Smoke control depends on the complete approved assembly and building strategy; a door that looks solid may still fail if seals, closer or frame are defective.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Field inspection', [
          'Check door closes fully and latches as designed; inspect closer, hinges, seals, frame, glazing, signage and visible damage.',
          'Never wedge or tie a fire door open, remove a closer, drill/cut the leaf or frame, fit unapproved hardware or paint over labels/seals.',
          'Check walls, ceilings, risers and service penetrations for unsealed gaps, damaged fire stopping, unauthorized cable/pipe openings or missing identification labels.',
          'Report doors that drag, fail to latch, have damaged seals, excessive gaps, broken glazing or missing certification/identification as applicable.',
          'Do not estimate fire rating by appearance. Confirm approved door schedule, labels, certificates and competent inspection.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Construction and change control', [
          'Before drilling, coring, cable pulling or pipe installation through a rated barrier, obtain approved method, location, compatible tested fire-stop system and responsible competent installer.',
          'Temporary openings must be controlled and restored promptly using an approved system; do not leave unsealed penetrations at shift end.',
          'Maintain a fire-stopping register or inspection evidence where required, including location, service type, system/product, installer, inspection and photo evidence.',
          'Changes to partitions, doors, ducts, ceilings or occupancy may affect the approved fire strategy and require designer/authority review.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Stop-work & emergency implications', [
          'Stop or restrict work that creates an unapproved opening in a fire barrier, disables a fire door, compromises a protected stair or leaves a critical penetration unsealed.',
          'Escalate missing or damaged fire doors, failed closers, wedged doors and compromised fire stopping to the responsible building/fire-safety manager.',
          'Do not alter door or barrier construction as a temporary fix without approval from the competent responsible party.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Site example', [
          'A subcontractor routes a new cable through a rated riser wall and leaves the annular gap open. Stop further work, protect the area, notify the responsible supervisor, install the approved compatible fire-stop system through a competent installer, inspect and record evidence before closure.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Detailed fire-door and compartment integrity controls', [
          'A fire compartment limits fire and smoke spread for the period and purpose defined by the approved fire strategy. Doors, walls, floors, ceilings, shafts, dampers, joints and service penetrations operate as a system; a single unapproved opening can compromise the boundary.',
          'Inspect each door for correct identification/label where specified, compatible frame and hardware, secure hinges, functioning closer, positive latching, intact seals, no damage or distortion, and unobstructed swing. Confirm it closes fully from its normal open position without being pulled shut by a person.',
          'Never wedge, tie or prop a fire door open. Where hold-open devices are approved, they must be part of the designed fire-alarm interface and release correctly on the specified signal; report any failure immediately.',
          'Before drilling, coring, cable pulling or pipe installation through a rated barrier, obtain approval, confirm the barrier type and required system, use a tested compatible fire-stop system, and have installation inspected before concealment. Ordinary foam or generic sealant is not an acceptable substitute unless specifically approved for that tested application.',
          'Maintain a penetration register showing location, barrier, service, approved product/system, installer, inspection, photographs and closure. Temporary openings require authorization, interim protection, a responsible owner and a firm restoration deadline.',
          'If a door will not latch, a seal is missing, or a barrier is breached, report and assess the affected compartment/escape route promptly; apply approved interim measures, restrict affected work/occupancy as required, and verify reinstatement before closing the defect.',
        ]),
      ],
      checklist: [
        'Fire doors are not wedged, tied open or obstructed.',
        'Door closes and latches; closer, hinges, seals and frame are intact.',
        'Door identification/labels and approved hardware are not damaged or removed.',
        'Fire-rated walls, ceilings and shafts have no unauthorized openings.',
        'Service penetrations have approved compatible fire stopping and evidence.',
        'Temporary openings and impairments are authorized, controlled and restored.',
        'Defects have responsible owner, interim control and verified closure.',
      ],
      requiredDocuments: [
        'Approved fire compartmentation drawings and fire-door schedule',
        'Fire door inspection and maintenance records',
        'Fire-stopping product/system approvals and installation records',
        'Penetration register, photographs and inspection/closure evidence',
        'Permit/method statement for drilling, coring or service penetrations',
        'Fire strategy change-control and authority approvals where applicable',
      ],
      interviewQuestions: [
        FireSafetyQuestion('Why must a fire door not be wedged open?', 'It can allow smoke and heat to spread into escape routes or other compartments, defeating the approved fire strategy.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('What is fire stopping?', 'A tested/approved system used to seal service openings and joints in fire-resisting construction to maintain the intended fire and smoke separation.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Can any sealant be used around a cable penetration?', 'No. Use a compatible tested/approved system suitable for the barrier, service, opening and required performance, installed by competent personnel.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('What should an HSE Officer do when a rated wall is penetrated without approval?', 'Stop or control the activity, notify the responsible manager, arrange approved competent reinstatement, inspect evidence and record closure.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Can fire rating be judged by appearance?', 'No. Verify approved drawings, product identification/certification, installation details and competent inspection.'),
      ],
      references: [
        'Current UAE Fire and Life Safety Code and applicable Civil Defence-approved fire strategy.',
        'Dubai Civil Defence emergency action plan guidance on keeping fire doors closed and escape routes clear: https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/eng/ANNEXUREB2EMERGENCYEVACUATION.pdf',
        'Approved fire-door schedule, tested fire-stop system documentation and manufacturer instructions.',
        'NFPA 80 / NFPA 101 — supplementary references only where adopted by authority or contract.',
      ],
    ),
    FireSafetyTopic(
      id: 'FLS-10',
      title: 'Fire Wardens, Emergency Team & Fire Drills',
      subtitle: 'Roles, communication, evacuation practice, headcount and learning',
      icon: Icons.groups_rounded,
      accent: Color(0xFF27866A),
      overview: 'A fire warden or designated emergency team member supports alarm response, orderly evacuation, area checks only where safe and assigned, assembly-point control and communication with the incident controller. Roles, staffing, training and drill frequency must follow the approved emergency plan, occupancy, applicable authority requirements and project procedures.',
      sections: [
        FireSafetySection('Roles & boundaries', [
          'Incident controller coordinates the response, interfaces with emergency services and decides operational priorities under the emergency plan.',
          'Fire warden guides people toward safe exits, reports route conditions, supports assigned areas and helps maintain assembly-point order.',
          'First-aid, security, facilities and technical teams perform only their assigned trained functions; do not create conflicting commands.',
          'Wardens must not enter smoke, search unsafe rooms, fight a developing fire or re-enter a building unless specifically trained, equipped, authorized and directed under the emergency plan.',
          'Every worker has a duty to raise alarm, follow evacuation instructions and report hazards or missing persons.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Training & communication', [
          'Training should cover alarm recognition, emergency number, safe route, assembly point, extinguisher-use limits, communication method and role-specific responsibilities.',
          'Include contractors, temporary workers, visitors and all shifts; use language and briefing methods workers understand.',
          'Keep current warden lists, area assignments, deputies, contact methods and shift coverage. Replace absent wardens through the approved arrangement.',
          'Use clear short messages: location, type of emergency, immediate hazard, route status, persons missing/injured and actions already taken.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Fire drill planning and conduct', [
          'Plan objectives, scope, scenario, participants, observers, communications, safety controls and coordination with building management/authority as required.',
          'Do not create real smoke, block exits, disable alarms or simulate dangerous conditions unless formally designed, authorized and safely controlled by competent specialists.',
          'Observe alarm recognition, evacuation flow, route usability, assistance, assembly-point discipline, headcount, communication and responder access.',
          'After the drill, conduct a debrief, record findings, assign action owners and due dates, implement improvements and verify closure.',
          'Drill frequency is governed by current authority, occupancy, project and emergency-plan requirements; do not assume one universal interval.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Incident accountability & recovery', [
          'At assembly, wardens report headcount, missing persons, last known location, injuries, hazards and route conditions to the incident controller.',
          'Never send staff back inside to search for missing persons; provide information to Civil Defence/emergency responders.',
          'Maintain incident and drill logs, training attendance, role assignment, lessons learned and corrective-action evidence.',
          'After an actual event, preserve relevant records, support authorized investigation and do not resume operations until formal clearance and risk controls are confirmed.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Stop-work & site example', [
          'If the designated warden is absent and no deputy/coverage is available for a high-occupancy activity, escalate and apply the approved contingency before starting or continuing.',
          'Example: a drill shows workers at a remote work front cannot hear the alarm. Record the location and conditions, notify the responsible fire-system/management team, assess interim warning controls and verify corrective action through a controlled retest.',
        ]),
        FireSafetySection('Detailed emergency-team duties and drill evaluation', [
          'The emergency organization chart should name the incident controller, deputies, fire wardens by area/shift, first-aid responders, security, facilities/system contacts and communication channels. Define who can order evacuation, contact emergency services, authorize re-entry and declare restoration.',
          'Before a shift or event, confirm role coverage, current contact lists, warden identification, radio/phone function, assembly-point assignments, visitor/contractor sign-in and arrangements for workers who need assistance. Brief changes and absent-role cover.',
          'Wardens guide evacuation only along safe routes, check assigned areas only where the plan permits and conditions remain safe, close doors if safe without delaying exit, report route obstructions and support assembly accountability. They do not enter smoke, conduct rescue beyond competence or fight a developing fire.',
          'A drill plan defines objective, scenario, scope, participants, observers, notification level, safety controls, emergency-service/building coordination, stop criteria and debrief method. Never block exits, create uncontrolled smoke, trigger unapproved alarm isolation or expose participants to real danger.',
          'Observers assess alarm recognition, time to initiate movement, route choice, congestion, assistance, warden communications, headcount accuracy, responder access and whether workers understand re-entry restrictions. Use the approved project criteria; do not present one universal evacuation-time target as law.',
          'Debrief promptly and record strengths, gaps, responsible owner, due date, interim risk control and evidence required. Verify corrective-action effectiveness by inspection, briefing or a repeat exercise; retain attendance, scenario, observer sheets and final report.',
          'At an actual emergency, report missing-person identity and last known location to the incident controller/emergency services. Do not send staff back into the building. Resume work only after formal clearance, system restoration and management authorization.',
        ]),
      ],
      checklist: [
        'Incident controller, wardens, deputies and communication roles are assigned for each shift.',
        'Wardens are trained, identifiable and know their safe role boundaries.',
        'Emergency number, alarm signal, routes and assembly points are briefed.',
        'Visitors and contractors are included in evacuation and headcount arrangements.',
        'Drill objectives, observers and safety controls are planned and approved.',
        'No unsafe simulation, blocked exit or unauthorized alarm isolation is used.',
        'Headcount, missing-person reporting and responder handover are tested.',
        'Drill findings have owners, due dates and verified closure.',
      ],
      requiredDocuments: [
        'Emergency Response Plan and emergency organization chart',
        'Fire warden/response team appointment and training records',
        'Shift coverage, area assignments and contact list',
        'Fire drill plan, attendance, observer checklist and report',
        'Headcount/visitor register and missing-person escalation procedure',
        'Corrective-action register, lessons learned and closure evidence',
      ],
      interviewQuestions: [
        FireSafetyQuestion('What is the role of a fire warden?', 'To support safe alarm response and evacuation, guide people within assigned safe boundaries, report conditions and support assembly-point accountability.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('Should a warden enter a smoke-filled room to search?', 'No. Wardens must not enter unsafe atmospheres or create additional casualties; report missing-person information to the incident controller and responders.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('What should a drill evaluate?', 'Alarm recognition, route usability, evacuation flow, assistance, headcount, communication, responder access and corrective-action learning.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('What if someone is missing at assembly?', 'Report their identity and last known location immediately to the incident controller; do not send untrained personnel back inside.'),
        FireSafetyQuestion('How are drill findings closed?', 'Assign an owner and due date, implement the correction, verify effectiveness and retain evidence in the corrective-action register.'),
      ],
      references: [
        'UAE Government fire emergency instructions and Civil Defence contact: https://u.ae/en/information-and-services/justice-safety-and-the-law/handling-emergencies',
        'Dubai Civil Defence emergency action plan annexure: https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/eng/ANNEXUREB2EMERGENCYEVACUATION.pdf',
        'Current authority-approved Emergency Response Plan, occupancy-specific requirements and project procedures.',
        'OSHA emergency action plan guidance — supplementary US reference only: https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.38',
      ],
    ),

